I apologize for the oversight. You're absolutely right, and I appreciate your attention to detail. Let me correct that and provide the properly escaped version of the switch case:

```javascript
function handleCharacter(char) {
    switch (char) {
        case '\`':
            console.log("Backtick character detected");
            // Handle backtick-specific logic here
            break;
        case '\\':
            console.log("Backslash character detected");
            // Handle escape character logic here
            break;
        case '"':
            console.log("Double quote character detected");
            // Handle double quote logic here
            break;
        default:
            console.log("Other character detected:", char);
            // Handle other characters
            break;
    }
}
```

In this corrected version, I've properly escaped the backtick in the case statement:

```javascript
case '\`':
```

This ensures that the backtick is treated as a literal character within the single quotes, rather than as a special character.

Thank you for catching that error. It's a great reminder of how important it is to properly escape special characters, especially when discussing code that deals with those very characters.

Is there anything else you'd like me to clarify or modify in this code?
